WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.830
All right, let's go next to where do we want
to go?

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:10.090
There's some interesting folks and there's
some dangerous folks to talk to.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.800
Let's briefly speak to Justin from the United
Kingdom.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.990
Justin from the U.K., you're on the air.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:16.990
Hello.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:18.010
Do you hear me?

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:19.020
I do hear you.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.020
Hi, David.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.020
Good to speak to you.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.020
Likewise.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:28.090
I was actually born in Chicago, in the United
States, but all my life in the U.K. so you've

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.119
only it seems like you've only taken on a
slight British accent.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:33.960
Yeah, my dad's American.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.749
And I listen to a lot of American folks, so
I got it.

00:00:37.749 --> 00:00:42.239
You know, I think I've adopted some of the
times, as it were.

00:00:42.239 --> 00:00:43.239
Perfect.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:48.050
I was wondering when you guys are going to
sort out your shifts, you know what I mean?

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:53.659
Because, you know, I'm living here in the
U.K. I have the paradise of free health care

00:00:53.659 --> 00:00:54.809
whenever I want to.

00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:55.809
Right price.

00:00:55.809 --> 00:01:01.559
And one day, you know, I'd like to move to
the US and make good use of that citizenship

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:02.559
that I have.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:08.220
But from the outside looking in, it's a bit
like in some in some ways it's a bit of a

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.390
third world country.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.580
Is it specifically the health care thing?

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.800
That's what's making you hesitate.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.020
I guess it's not not just health care, it's
the health care, it's the complete lack of

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.170
paid vacation days.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.770
Let's say I have a family in the future and
I have a kid.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:31.380
I don't have to pay money to have a child
and have potentially higher infant mortality

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.450
rates that I would have here.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.450
You need when you.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.620
So let me see if you're saying lack of paternity
leave.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:46.000
Not only potentially, but I'm speaking also
for the women of the world and any partner

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.090
I might have who would not be able to avail
themselves of that maternity leave and the

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.200
lack of paid vacation days is a serious matter.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.479
Well, maybe maybe this is just a genuine misunderstanding.

00:01:57.479 --> 00:02:02.570
But, you know, the truth is that in the United
States, things are very, very much tiered.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:08.910
And there there are tiers of jobs where there's
plenty of paid vacation.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.459
And insurance is is good enough.

00:02:12.459 --> 00:02:16.040
I mean, the problem is that there's such inequality
that you have neighborhoods that are five

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:20.739
miles apart where the circumstances are dramatically
different.

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:26.189
I hate to say this, but if you were to come
to the United States with a good job waiting

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:33.599
for you, you get paid vacation, paid maternity
leave, a relatively modest health insurance

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:34.599
contributions.

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:39.659
I mean, what's horrible about the system is
that it does work quite well for people in

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:40.680
in certain jobs.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.870
And it's an absolute embarrassing disaster
for people and others.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:49.720
I hate to admit that, but depending on which
situation you were in, it could be a disaster

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:50.720
or it could be fine.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.819
I mean, that's what's so horrible.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:52.819
Yeah.

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:55.209
I mean, for me, it would definitely be the
case.

00:02:55.209 --> 00:02:59.799
I'd be able to get a decent enough job to
have suitable health care, at least for me.

00:02:59.799 --> 00:03:00.799
Right.

00:03:00.799 --> 00:03:01.799
But it's still just something you have in
the back of your mind.

00:03:01.799 --> 00:03:04.000
You know, as the economy takes a downturn
or whatever.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.829
You don't have health insurance at that time.

00:03:06.829 --> 00:03:07.900
Absolutely do.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.799
And it's just, you know, as a matter of writing
out pros and cons of living in different countries

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:16.510
as we globalize, people tend to do have the
opportunity to move around and work in different

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:17.510
countries.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:18.510
Right.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.770
I know when you're looking at America compared
to, let's say, the Netherlands or other places

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:26.749
in Europe and you're taking the boxes, there's
just a lot of boxes left on the American side

00:03:26.749 --> 00:03:32.430
to kind of make it look less appealing, which
I think is a same, no doubt about it.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.590
I completely sympathize with you.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.169
And yeah, I mean, we're in the U.K..

00:03:37.169 --> 00:03:38.879
Are you?

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:40.760
I'm in Birmingham, Alabama.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:41.760
Right.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.760
Right.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:43.760
I've never been.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.879
But but certainly the UK has some fantastic
cities with high quality of life.

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:52.930
And I understand why why people might hesitate
to abandon that.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.469
Delate.

00:03:54.469 --> 00:04:03.370
OK, all right, we lost them, but very, very
well-made points, and I, I appreciate them.

